From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: =?UTF-8?q?Berkay=20K=C3=B6sebay?= <brkosebay@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sat, 21 Nov 2026 12:07:00 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] drm/imagination: apple: Render on the A8 (T7000)

Give the A8's firmware geometry and fragment jobs:

- The parameter manager, one set per VM, as Apple's fresh queue: a
  control block with 36 state records in the root memory and the pool's
  page list in slices of 32 in the shared memory (at the VM's slot's
  place in each), the PM's own tables in the VM, and the free-list page
  stack: a page number array mapped in the VM, filled on the CPU, which
  the firmware loads into the PM at every TA start. Without it the TA
  faulted in the PM's context. The tables and the stack lie behind the
  general heap, where userspace cannot map; the pool's pages are never
  mapped. A scene uses S[1], as Apple's first frame; the set is
  built for each start of the firmware and again after a scene whose
  fragment job never came. Apple's A8 kext sends no PM command.
- The pool's size. Apple's fresh queue starts with 2 blocks (64 pages,
  256 KiB) and grows inside a scene ("Synchronous PB Grow" records) and
  with partial renders; this driver has neither. With 2 blocks, scenes
  of 16384 small triangles at 256x256 and a lit 3072-triangle sphere
  never finished their geometry job: the firmware's out-of-memory
  handler marks the TA and waits, and its watchdog skips an engine with
  PM_OUT_OF_MEMORY pending, so nothing is reported. A new VM's pool now
  starts at a8_pm_blocks (default 32 blocks, 4 MiB; at most 61, what one
  set's list holds). Growing a live VM's pool from the firmware's usage
  report between scenes, as the A9 and Apple's
  growPoolFromFirmwareFeedback do, is behind a8_pm_grow, off by default:
  it rebuilds the set under the same PM id, and whether a TA start
  reloads the stack then is not known yet. Off, the usage words are
  logged at most once a second per VM.
- Region headers as Apple's mirrored ring: userspace maps one buffer
  twice back to back; a frame takes the next of its 16 slices (the
  target's region headers rounded up to 4 KiB), never cleared. The ring
  and its cursor live in the HWRT data set (struct pvr_hwrt_dataset
  apple_rgn), as Apple keeps the cursor in each render target's RTM:
  set when its first geometry job is prepared at the ioctl, gone with
  the data set. A queued job holds its data set, so its ring is there
  when the job worker submits it, however many targets are in flight.
  (A per-VM table of four rings refused thousands of jobs under vkmark
  and glmark2 through Zink: a later prepare took the slot of a target
  whose geometry job was still queued.) A data set whose region headers
  are not such a ring is refused at its first geometry job.
- The records: the A9's fill at the A8's offsets with Apple's A8 values
  where they differ (no TA scratch base, resume or TA PDS words; no
  scale words; +0x3c8 0x22000; the 3D ISP and output words for one
  sample and an output width of 2), and the render targets' PBE register
  words from the Rogue command at Apple's three places. Then the
  kernel's links and Apple's microsequences: TA start 0x23, 3D start
  0x25 with 23 arguments, the 3D wait block, both finishes.
- The TA and 3D channels in Apple's form (its header and a 0x144-entry
  ring), one record each: TA one pointer-ring entry, 3D the barrier
  command on the TA's finish stamp and then the record. Apple's kicks:
  TA DM0, 3D DM1.

The firmware keeps one scene at a time: a geometry job waits for the
last fragment job, a fragment job for its geometry job. One sample and
single-layer targets only.

Assisted-by: LLM
---
